===Etymology===
According to Merriam-Webster Unabridged and OED from rare and obsolete French ''double entendre'', which literally meant "double meaning" and was used in the senses of "double understanding" or "ambiguity" but acquired its current suggestive twist after being first used in English in 1673 by John Dryden.<ref name=grammarphobia>[https://www.grammarphobia.com/blog/2011/10/double-entendre.html The Grammarphobia Blog: Double entendre]</ref> From {{der|en|fr|double||double}} + {{m|fr|entendre||to mean, to [[understand]]}}. (The phrase has not been used in French for centuries and would be ungrammatical in modern French.) No exact equivalent exists in French, whose similar expressions [[double entente]] and [[double sens]] don't have the suggestiveness of the English expression.<ref name=grammarphobia/>

